Short RFeflections:
1) Relate bit-depth to visible banding/posterization you observed.
Bit-depth determines the number of intensity levels an image can represent. 
8-bit = 256 intensity levels
6-bit = 64
4-bit = 16
Lower bit-depth causes loss of intensity information, resulting in visible banding and posterization.

2) How does contrast stretching change the histogram and visibility of details?
Contrast stretching spreads pixel intensity values across a wider range. 
This changes the histogram distribution and improves the difference between dark and bright areas. 
As a result, details become easier to see, especially in low-contrast images.

3) Explain why aggressive downsampling causes aliasing (reference Nyquist).
Aggressive downsampling reduces the number of pixels used to represent an image. 
If the sampling frequency is too low then the high-frequency details cannot be represented correctly. 
According to the Nyquist theorem, the sampling frequency must be at least twice the highest signal frequency or else aliasing and distortion can occur.
